{
 "id": "Vectors-DotProduct-CauchySchwarz",
 "title_en": "The Cauchy-Schwarz and triangle inequalities",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "The **Cauchy-Schwarz inequality** is a fundamental inequality satisfied by the dot product. It is given in the following proposition.",
    "sha": "50dac17da30d",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "proposition",
   "title": {
    "en": "Cauchy-Schwarz inequality",
    "sha": "3c57397ed673",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "The dot product satisfies the inequality",
      "sha": "e8981b1c2f13",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\abs{\\vect{u}\\dotprod \\vect{v}}\\leq \\norm{\\vect{u}} \\norm{\\vect{v}} \\tag{1}"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Furthermore equality is obtained if and only if one of $\\vect{u}$ or $\\vect{v}$ is a scalar multiple of the other.",
      "sha": "0fda53954dd4",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "proof",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "First note that if $\\vect{u}=\\vect{0}$, then both sides of (1) are equal to zero, and so the inequality holds in this case. Therefore, we will assume in what follows that $\\vect{u}\\neq \\vect{0}$. Define a function of $t\\in \\R$ by",
      "sha": "8c9c07c6fd85",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "f(t) =(t\\vect{u}+\\vect{v}) \\dotprod (t\\vect{u}+\\vect{v})."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Then by Proposition “Properties of the dot product”, $f(t) \\geq 0$ for all $t\\in \\R$. Also from Proposition “Properties of the dot product”, we have",
      "sha": "b7b648b45774",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\begin{array}{rcl}f(t)\n    &=& t\\vect{u}\\dotprod (t\\vect{u}+\\vect{v})\n        + \\vect{v}\\dotprod (t\\vect{u}+\\vect{v}) \\\\\n    &=& t^2\\vect{u}\\dotprod \\vect{u}+t(\\vect{u}\\dotprod \\vect{v})\n        + t \\vect{v}\\dotprod \\vect{u}\n        + \\vect{v}\\dotprod \\vect{v} \\\\\n    &=& t^2\\norm{\\vect{u}}^2\n        + 2t(\\vect{u}\\dotprod \\vect{v})\n        + \\norm{\\vect{v}}^2.\\end{array}"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "This means the graph of $y=f(t)$ is a parabola which opens upwards and is never negative. It follows that this function has at most one root. From the quadratic formula, we know that a quadratic function $at^2+bt+c$ has one or zero roots if and only if $b^2-4ac\\leq 0$. Applying this reasoning to the function $f(t)$, we obtain",
      "sha": "f494b039f372",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "(2(\\vect{u}\\dotprod\\vect{v}))^2-4\\norm{\\vect{u}}^2\\norm{\\vect{v}}^2\n    \\leq 0,"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "which is equivalent to $\\abs{\\vect{u}\\dotprod \\vect{v}} \\leq \\norm{\\vect{u}} \\norm{\\vect{v}}$.",
      "sha": "2f3b449158ec",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "An important consequence of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is the so-called **triangle inequality** triangle inequality!in Rn@in $\\R^n$, which states that the length of one side of a triangle is less than or equal the sum of the lengths of the two other sides.",
    "sha": "e9249974e323",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "proposition",
   "title": {
    "en": "Triangle inequality",
    "sha": "e26f833d1f48",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "For\\/ $\\vect{u},\\vect{v}\\in \\R^n$, we have",
      "sha": "76a436118aee",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\norm{\\vect{u} + \\vect{v}} \\leq \\norm{\\vect{u}} + \\norm{\\vect{v}}. \\tag{2}"
    },
    {
     "k": "figure",
     "env": "tikzpicture",
     "caption": null
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "proof",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "By properties of the dot product and the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have",
      "sha": "70ca2c662c5d",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\begin{array}{rcl}\\norm{\\vect{u}+\\vect{v}}^2\n    &=& (\\vect{u}+\\vect{v})\\dotprod(\\vect{u}+\\vect{v}) \\\\\n    &=& (\\vect{u}\\dotprod\\vect{u})+(\\vect{u}\\dotprod\\vect{v})+(\\vect{v}\\dotprod\\vect{u})+(\\vect{v}\\dotprod\\vect{v}) \\\\\n    &=& \\norm{\\vect{u}}^2+2(\\vect{u}\\dotprod\\vect{v})+\\norm{\\vect{v}}^2 \\\\\n    &\\leq& \\norm{\\vect{u}}^2+2\\abs{\\vect{u}\\dotprod\\vect{v}}+\\norm{\\vect{v}}^2 \\\\\n    &\\leq& \\norm{\\vect{u}}^2+2\\norm{\\vect{u}}\\norm{\\vect{v}}+\\norm{\\vect{v}}^2 \\\\\n    &=& (\\norm{\\vect{u}}+\\norm{\\vect{v}})^2.\\end{array}"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Therefore,",
      "sha": "236a32def63c",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\norm{\\vect{u}+\\vect{v}}^2\\leq(\\norm{\\vect{u}}+\\norm{\\vect{v}})^2."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Taking square roots of both sides, we obtain (2).",
      "sha": "5376a09157de",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "example",
   "title": {
    "en": "Triangle inequality",
    "sha": "e26f833d1f48",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Use the triangle inequality to show",
      "sha": "9cc6fdf77c59",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\norm{\\vect{u}} -\\norm{\\vect{v}} \\leq \\norm{\\vect{u}-\\vect{v}}"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "holds for all vectors $\\vect{u},\\vect{v}\\in\\R^n$.",
      "sha": "1c5b7af22f92",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "solution",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "We have",
      "sha": "a2214cd5b1a4",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\norm{\\vect{u}}\n    = \\norm{(\\vect{u}-\\vect{v})+\\vect{v}} \\\\\n    \\leq \\norm{\\vect{u}-\\vect{v}}+\\norm{\\vect{v}},"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "where we have used the triangle inequality in the last step. Note that this is an inequality between real numbers. Bringing $\\norm{\\vect{v}}$ to the other side of the equation, we have",
      "sha": "a935cee57e15",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\norm{\\vect{u}} - \\norm{\\vect{v}} \\leq \\norm{\\vect{u}-\\vect{v}}."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "exercises": [],
 "title_zh": "柯西—施瓦茨不等式与三角不等式"
}
